_model: proposition
---
title: Proposition 29
---
order: 29
---
short_label: III.29
---
statement: In equal circles straight lines that cut off equal circumferences are equal.
---
proof:

Let *ABC* and *DEF* be equal circles, and in them let equal circumferences *BGC* and *EHF* be cut off.  Join the straight lines *BC* and *EF.*

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="400" height="220" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="III.29 diagram" src="propIII28.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "III.29",
        elements: [
            "K;point;free;110,110",
            "C;point;free;110,30;black",
            "ABC;circle;radius;K,C",
            "A;point;circleSlider;ABC,60,170",
            "B;point;circleSlider;ABC,160,170",
            "L;point;free;290,110",
            "DEF;circle;radius;L,K,C",
            "D;point;circleSlider;DEF,240,170",
            "F;point;circleSlider;DEF,290,30",
            "E;point;similar;L,D,K,A,B",
            "G;point;circleSlider;ABC,110,190",
            "H;point;circleSlider;DEF,290,190",
            "ABK;polygon;triangle;A,B,K;0;0;black;random",
            "DEL;polygon;triangle;D,E,L;0;0;black;random"
        ],
    });
</script>
</figure>

I say that *BC* equals *EF.*

Take the centers *K* and *L* of the circles.  Join *BK, KC, EL,* and *LF.* [!just III.1]

Now, since the circumference *BGC* equals the circumference *EHF,* the angle *BKC* also equals the angle *ELF.* [!just III.27]

And, since the circles *ABC* and *DEF* are equal, the radii are also equal, therefore the two sides *BK* and *KC* equal the two sides *EL* and *LF,* and they contain equal angles, therefore the base *BC* equals the base *EF.* [!just I.4]

Therefore *in equal circles straight lines that cut off equal circumferences are equal.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
<br clear="all">
---
body:

This proposition is used in @IV.11 and @IV.15.
